          "This chapter follows industrialisation as a long, uneven change in how goods were made, sold, and worked on. Before factories dominated, merchants organised production in rural homes: artisans spun or wove goods for wider markets. This proto-industrial system let merchants increase production without immediately bringing every worker into a factory.",
          "In Britain, inventions changed textile production: John Kay's flying shuttle improved weaving in 1733, James Hargreaves's spinning jenny increased spinning capacity in 1764, and water- and steam-powered machines later supported mills. Factories concentrated machinery, workers, time, and supervision. Yet hand production continued because skilled work, custom orders, and changing demand did not always suit machines.",
          "In India, colonial policy and global competition shaped industrial change. Company agents called gomasthas supervised weavers, while imported machine-made cloth competed with Indian handloom textiles. Indian entrepreneurs also invested in mills: a cotton mill opened in Bombay in 1854 and a jute mill was established at Rishra in Bengal in 1855. The chapter therefore connects technology to markets, labour, colonial power, and enterprise. Industrialisation was not a simple story of machines replacing hands; its effects differed across workers, regions, producers, and consumers."
